rewritten-to-match
Same interpreter: each run compiles the program text into the eight command bytes and a bracket jump table, then interprets it on a 30,000-cell tape that grows when the pointer passes the end (a new cell is set to 0). The reference's match on the command is an if-chain (the language has no switch), its u8 and u32 wrapping is masking, and the program text is read byte by byte from the request input (the text is copied into a state array in prepare, as the reference holds a String). The warmup program is run 1000 times before the timed run, as in the reference. Other stores are range-checked; state is unlogged, so nothing is saved for a rollback. Scratch state rebuilt every run (the reference's locals: src, cmds, jumps, stack, tape) is not logged (state is unlogged by default, so no undo log, as a Rust local has none).
